 1 big thing: Anthropic gets hit by AI trade uncertainty 
 
 By Madison Mills 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Anthropic officially surpassed SpaceX to pull off the largest IPO in history after underwriters exercised the greenshoe option to purchase more shares, but the stock tumbled in the early hours today amid a broader rout of tech stocks.

 Why it matters: Anthropic may not be fully immune to Wall Street's broader tech uncertainty.

 State of play: Anthropic shares jumped 17% on its first day of trading, but the stock fell over 12% in premarket trading this morning.

 The AI company ultimately raised $93.5 billion from the IPO, marking the largest stock sale ever, yielding a $1.7 trillion valuation and making the company more valuable than Meta. Yes, but: The optimism hit a wall this morning as the stock tumbled.

 The drop could be due to selling pressure from employees who are able to sell shares immediately, as well as investors who are looking to return capital to their LPs, Brandon Ross, partner at LightShed Partners, said on Bloomberg TV. Zoom in: Some employees were told to sell on day one to decrease their exposure to the company and the tech trade as a whole, as uncertainty about the AI-driven market rises, sources tell Axios.

 Unlike with other IPOs, eligible Anthropic employees can sell up to 10% of their vested shares on day one, totaling 315 million shares . Typically, companies have a 180-day lockup period. What they're saying: Anthropic's early investors aren't trying to "time the market" with the stock or its IPO.

 "You just have this long-term mentality. It is important to us as a company that we are in the public markets, and [Anthropic] executed to that really, really well," Lightspeed partner Guru Chahal told Axios. Sebastian Duesterhoeft, a partner at Lightspeed, told Axios that Anthropic has a history of overdelivering despite challenging circumstances: It wasn't as well funded early on as competitors, it started later, and, now, it's gone public in the midst of a tech sell-off. Lightspeed's initial $3.5 billion Anthropic investment is now valued at about $25 billion. The big picture: Anthropic is in the early innings for enterprise adoption, many investors told Axios.

 "This year, an enterprise that was spending $1 with them on average last year is spending $9 with them this year. That's a 900% net dollar retention. That's the number to focus on," Chahal said. The bottom line: Anthropic is now public, meaning days of wild swings for the stock are just beginning.

 
 
