 2. Scoop: Cairncross out, Davies in as cyber boss 
 
 By Alex Isenstadt , Maria Curi and Marc Caputo 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Davies has been the Pentagon's chief information officer. Photo: Tom Williams/CQ-Roll Call, Inc. via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 The White House named Kirsten A. Davies to be its new national cyber director yesterday as President Trump overhauls his AI team.

 Why it matters: Davies, who currently serves as the Pentagon's chief information officer , has more experience in the field than the outgoing director , Sean Cairncross , who stepped down yesterday.

 Axios was the first to report his departure. Behind the scenes: Cairncross' departure was described as amicable by two sources.

 But two White House officials said Trump's new chief of staff, Stephen Miller, sought the change and pushed out Cairncross because, one said, he "lacked the strong cyber credentials to perform the job." The search for Cairncross' replacement began two weeks ago, a third source said. Zoom out: The position requires Senate confirmation. Davies previously was cleared by the Senate as the Defense Department's chief information officer. 

 "Kirsten has the credentials to excel in this position and has already been Senate-confirmed," an administration official said. At the Pentagon, Davies has been the top adviser to Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth "on all matters relating to information management, information technology, cybersecurity, and information assurance," according to her bio.

 Before serving in the Trump administration, Davies worked for Unilever and "led global cyber resilience for one of the world's largest consumer goods enterprises," her bio says. 
 
