 3. Meta rethinks climate goal as emissions soar 
 
 By Amy Harder 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Gabriella Turrisi/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Meta's greenhouse gas emissions surged 45% last year amid its AI infrastructure boom, prompting the tech giant to reconsider its goal of reaching net-zero emissions by 2030.

 Why it matters: Other tech giants have reported rising emissions as they race to build data centers, but Meta is going a step further by explicitly reconsidering its climate deadline.

 Driving the news: The just-released sustainability report of the tech giant, one of North America's largest data center operators , shows just how much growth in the AI industry is outpacing any efficiency gains.

 Meta executives said they were "re-evaluating" the 2030 goal, first announced in 2020 when the company was still known as Facebook and before the generative AI boom. They didn't specify what changes they're considering or when they'll make a decision. By the numbers: Meta's electricity use jumped 35%, in line with other tech giants. But Meta's emissions increase of 45% is far higher than those of peers Google (18%), Amazon (16%) and Microsoft (25%).

 What they're saying: "When we set our net zero emissions goal, we anticipated steady growth, falling clean energy costs, and changes in the broader system that would enable progress at the pace we envisioned," wrote Rachel Peterson, vice president of data centers, and Blair Swedeen, global head of net zero and sustainability.

 "The reality has been far more complex." Friction point: Meta's report was also revealing about water, a more immediate concern for many communities near data centers.

 The company's direct water consumption fell 4%, but consumption in highly water-stressed regions rose 24%. The estimated water consumption associated with generating its electricity — an often-overlooked environmental impact — rose 37% before accounting for its clean-energy purchases. Natural gas plants typically use substantial amounts of water for cooling, meaning Meta's growing reliance on gas could increase its indirect water footprint even as its data centers become more water-efficient. What we're watching: Meta's reconsideration of its climate deadline raises the question of whether other tech giants will eventually follow suit as the AI boom continues to strain their environmental commitments.

 
 
